<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- harbor:template-only
     THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE (harbor#630). Do not cp it: @HOME@ and the other placeholders in it would be
     installed as literal text, and launchd loads such a job and then fails every time it runs. Render it:

       sh ~/port/harbor/bin/deploy-launchd-render.sh ~/port/harbor/launchd/com.harbor.status.usage-sessions.plist > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.harbor.status.usage-sessions.plist

     A cp line in the comment below predates the template. It is kept so the rendered file stays byte-identical
     to the one already installed: follow this block, not that line. This block is not installed;
     bin/deploy-launchd-render.sh drops it. -->
<!-- harbor#185 — the PER-SESSION half of the same ledger.

     EVERY ARGUMENT FOR THE DAILY JOB APPLIES UNCHANGED and is not restated
     here: read com.harbor.status.usage.plist for why this is a launchd job and not a
     Stop hook, why stdout is declared as well as stderr, why the ledger is
     host-partitioned, and why an agent cannot install it. This file records only
     what differs.

     WHY A SECOND JOB RATHER THAN A SECOND FLAG ON THE FIRST. `--sessions`
     selects a VIEW: it reports and applies the per-session ledger, and without
     it the daily one. That is deliberate — the two ledgers share no key, so one
     invocation cannot merge both without a reader having to filter before it can
     sum. launchd takes one argument vector per job, so two views are two jobs.
     The alternative, `sh -c "a && b"`, buries the script name inside a shell
     string where usage-aggregate-test.sh's U-18 can no longer read it, and the
     assertion that the job still points at a shipped script is worth more than
     one fewer file.

     WHY IT MATTERS THAT THIS RUNS AT ALL. harbor#184's four levers — batching
     calls, reading slices, delegating to subagents, routing to a cheaper model —
     each need a before-and-after on the same measure, and the measure is these
     rows. Cost here is cache reads, which are context size MULTIPLIED BY call
     count: both are per-session properties and neither appears in a
     (day, model, entrypoint) row. Unscheduled, the measure exists and no history
     accumulates, which is the state the daily ledger was in until 2026-08-09.

     TWENTY MINUTES AFTER THE DAILY JOB, not at the same minute. Both walk every
     transcript, and two full walks racing each other on one disk buys nothing;
     the offset also keeps the two /tmp logs readable as separate events.

     Operator steps (an agent is blocked from all three by the floor):

       sh ~/port/harbor/bin/deploy-runtime-install.sh --live --apply
       cp launchd/com.harbor.status.usage-sessions.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
       launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.harbor.status.usage-sessions.plist

     Then see it work rather than assume it:

       launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.harbor.status.usage-sessions
       tail -3 /tmp/com.harbor.status.usage-sessions.out          # did the job run
       wc -l ~/.claude/MEMORY/USAGE/*/sessions.jsonl       # did the ledger grow

     TEMPLATE — launchd does not expand $HOME: replace @HOME@ with your
     home dir on install. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.harbor.status.usage-sessions</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/sh</string>
    <string>@HOME@/.claude/bin/status-usage-aggregate.sh</string>
    <string>--sessions</string>
    <string>--apply</string>
    <string>--live</string>
  </array>
  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <dict>
    <key>Hour</key><integer>4</integer>
    <key>Minute</key><integer>37</integer>
  </dict>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Background</string>
  <key>LowPriorityIO</key><true/>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key><string>/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:@HARBOR_SCOPE_LOCAL_BIN@</string>
  </dict>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/com.harbor.status.usage-sessions.out</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/com.harbor.status.usage-sessions.err</string>
</dict>
</plist>
